{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "mtg-grimoire-relay",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-27",
  // One Durable Object per pairing group, addressed by the group id. No R2, no KV: KV allows
  // 1 000 writes/day on the free plan, which this would spend in an afternoon.
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [{ "name": "GROUP", "class_name": "Group" }],
  },
  "migrations": [
    {
      "tag": "v1",
      // `new_sqlite_classes` and NOT `new_classes`: Durable Objects are on the free plan
      // **SQLite-backed only**, and the key-value backend is a paid feature.
      "new_sqlite_classes": ["Group"],
    },
  ],
  // The entitlement store — `relay/schema.sql` is its shape, applied with
  // `npx wrangler d1 execute mtg-grimoire-relay --remote --file=./schema.sql`.
  //
  // **`database_id` is real and the database exists** — this comment called it a placeholder
  // until 2026-08-30, and `relay/README.md` step 1 said the committed value was `<set on create>`.
  // Neither was true, and the pair of them is why the runbook now opens by probing the live host
  // instead of asserting anything. Only `npx wrangler d1 create mtg-grimoire-relay` can produce a
  // real id; inventing a plausible uuid here would be a value that gets copied into documentation
  // and deployed against.
  //
  // D1 rather than KV: KV allows 1 000 writes/day on the free plan. These rows are read rarely
  // and written rarely, and the hot path — the auth gate in `index.ts` — touches no storage at
  // all, which is the whole reason the access token is signed rather than looked up.
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "mtg-grimoire-relay",
      "database_id": "5e0381b0-d2aa-4861-a725-26ca18ae00ef",
    },
  ],
  // The webhook is primary and this is the backstop: a webhook Patreon failed to deliver would
  // otherwise leave a cancelled membership syncing for ever, and an expired grace window
  // (spec 7.2) has no webhook of its own to close it.
  //
  // **Hourly, and it was daily at `0 3` until 2026-09-28.** One invocation may make fifty
  // external subrequests on the free plan — reported, not verified; `claim.ts`'s
  // `RECONCILE_BUDGET` says where from — and a subject costs two, so `claim.ts`'s `reconcile`
  // reaches at most `RECONCILE_BUDGET` (20) subjects a pass and each is due again only after
  // `RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MS` (20 h). The daily ceiling is therefore 20 × 24 = 480 subjects rather
  // than 20 — and on a relay smaller than that, most hourly invocations find nothing due and end
  // after one read. `index.ts`'s `scheduled` sweeps the pairing rendezvous after it, D1 only,
  // which is as happy hourly as daily.
  //
  // Still one trigger of the free plan's five, and still minute `0`: the share Worker's lapse pass
  // fires at `30 3` so the two never contend on this D1, and an hourly `0` keeps that true.
  "triggers": { "crons": ["0 * * * *"] },
  // Public values, committed on purpose — spec 9 puts both on this side of the line, and each
  // is on the wire of every request that uses it.
  //
  // **`RELAY_BASE` must equal `entitlement::RELAY_BASE` in the Rust byte for byte.** The OAuth
  // redirect URI is built from it here and from that constant there, and Patreon compares
  // redirect URIs exactly — at the authorize request and again at the code exchange. A trailing
  // slash on either side fails the exchange with `invalid_grant`, which says nothing about a
  // path.
  //
  // **`PATREON_CLIENT_ID` and `PATREON_CAMPAIGN_ID` are the real values**, public for the same
  // reason and so `vars` rather than secrets. `PATREON_CLIENT_ID` must equal
  // `entitlement::PATREON_CLIENT_ID` in the Rust byte for byte: the app builds the authorize URL
  // and the relay builds the code exchange, and Patreon compares the two. `required()` in
  // `patreon.ts` still turns a binding removed from here into a 500 that names it. The three that
  // are never committed, set with `wrangler secret put`, are `PATREON_CLIENT_SECRET`,
  // `PATREON_WEBHOOK_SECRET` and `RELAY_HMAC_KEY`.
  // The limits on the five routes a caller reaches with no token — runbook step 8, and
  // `src/ratelimit.ts` is the reasoning. Each of those routes reads D1 before it can refuse
  // anything, and a limit refuses the caller ahead of that read. **It does not keep the request
  // off the account's 100,000-a-day budget**: the Worker has already been invoked to ask, and a
  // rule in front of the Worker needs a zone, which `workers.dev` has none of.
  //
  // One binding per limit, because a binding carries exactly one: `/claim`; `/token` and
  // `/rotate`; `/keys` and the pairing rendezvous. Per client address, per 60 seconds — `period`
  // takes 10 or 60 and nothing else. `namespace_id` is an integer of our own choosing, unique to
  // this account, and the bucket a count is kept in: reusing one across two bindings would have
  // them spend each other's budget.
  //
  // ⚠️ **`src/ratelimit.ts`'s `LIMITS` repeats these three names and numbers**, and
  // `ratelimit.test.ts` reads this file to hold them together. A name that drifts is a binding
  // the Worker reads as `undefined`, which it treats as "no limit" on purpose — so the deploy
  // would succeed, every probe would answer, and nothing would be limited.
  "ratelimits": [
    { "name": "RL_CLAIM", "namespace_id": "1001", "simple": { "limit": 10, "period": 60 } },
    { "name": "RL_MINT", "namespace_id": "1002", "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 60 } },
    { "name": "RL_READ", "namespace_id": "1003", "simple": { "limit": 240, "period": 60 } },
  ],
  "vars": {
    "RELAY_BASE": "https://mtg-grimoire-relay.denmark-east.workers.dev",
    "PATREON_CLIENT_ID": "UFkUESN45GFyy36WQBaOpedWwFvtcOfujPZz_s8Yhu54z4I7-eA8Zg9lv-FEXqV4",
    "PATREON_CAMPAIGN_ID": "16694056",
  },
  "observability": { "enabled": true },
}
